CORE
main.cpp
--cpp14
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ generic lambda: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
N5008 [expr.prim.lambda.closure]/4, [temp.inst]/11: the function call
operator of a generic lambda is a member function template, instantiated
only for the argument types it is called with (auto = double here).  The
front end also type-checked the body with `signed int' for auto: it
instantiated fmt<int>, whose call h(int) is ambiguous, and dropped that body
("C++ front-end dropped the body of function 'fmt<signed_int>'", bin53).
For a lambda lowered to a closure with a template operator() that trial is
no longer made (cpp_typecheckt::typecheck_expr_lambda).  libstdc++ 13
<format> still drops __formatter_fp::format (format:1422), reached through
_M_format_arg's visitor, which is not closure-lowered (it captures `this'
in a member function).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept
this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
